      "role": "user",
      "content": "i hear that, but it feels a bit abstract when you're the one looking at the books. if i have to raise wages by $5 an hour just to keep people, i might have to cut two staff members or raise my coffee prices so high that nobody in town comes in anymore. doesn't that kind of negate the 'extra spending power' they talk about? it feels like the theory sounds good until it hits the reality of a small shop's budget. what's the actual data on whether these raises help or hurt small town economies in the long run?",
